    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Many families struggle with end-of-life planning conversations for a variety of personal, cultural, and religious reasons. Those who want to have such conversations can be uncertain about how to approach the topic; this difficulty can be further exacerbated when there is a generational gap. As a result, many families avoid these conversations altogether. However, putting off the conversation until it cannot be avoided (e.g., in times of medical emergency), makes the conversation and necessary decisions even more stressful. Having these vital discussions earlier helps ensure people can live life the way they want, through the end.  It also lowers the burden on caretakers and other loved ones. \r\n\r\nThis report describes Lifely, a new website (planwithlifely.com) designed to help adults in their mid 20s to late 30s prepare for, have, and reflect on end-of-life planning conversations with their parents. Lifely provides a suite of exercises to help families come to the best possible conversations and solutions for everyone. Lifely leverages expert perspectives and helps users focus on how people want to live (rather than on death). By encouraging users to have these conversations sooner, Lifely makes having end-of-life planning conversations more personal and joyful.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
